      "math_academy_id": "q-11781",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/e588a9250583251ba2fd85b789eb3a856970fb6e7044289257a4ed97e3bbf1a0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
