    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115432",
      "knowledge_point_id": "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(2,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(1)y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{x}^{2}=4y",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}y",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{x}^{2}=-y",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{x}^{2}=16y",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{x}^{2}=y",
