  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115451",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/970db64a0ddd325993323511ad4ae754ce64f64d591877bfa0ac908874e2393f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =2(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(x-2)^{2}=4(y-1)",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(x+2)^{2}=(y-1)",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "easy",
