  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115513",
      "knowledge_point_id": "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,0)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-4).$",
      "worked_solution": "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 9 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
