  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953780",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122888",
      "knowledge_point_id": "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve",
      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-10,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+15.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t))}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
